WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.415
When I came on deck the next morning, the island looked entirely different, a bit colorless and sad.

2
00:00:07.416 --> 00:00:15.648
The trees, which covered most of the surface, looked gray, with only streaks of yellow beach sand breaking up the dreariness.

3
00:00:15.649 --> 00:00:23.767
The three hills were oddly shaped spires of naked rock above the tall, dark pines and low trees.

4
00:00:23.768 --> 00:00:34.186
The Spyglass towered about four hundred feet above everything else, with sheer stone sides and a top that seemed flat enough to be a statue’s pedestal.

5
00:00:34.187 --> 00:00:40.703
There was no wind, so we were stopped in the ocean, about half a mile from the coast.

6
00:00:40.704 --> 00:00:44.993
Water rolled across the Hispaniola ’s deck from the rough

7
00:00:44.994 --> 00:00:51.838
waves, and as the rudder banged to and fro, the whole ship creaked and groaned like some kind of

8
00:00:51.839 --> 00:01:00.741
factory. Though I was a decent sailor when we were moving forward, being rolled about on deck like a bottle in the muggy heat made me

9
00:01:00.742 --> 00:01:06.422
seasick. From the first look, I hated the very thought of Treasure

10
00:01:06.423 --> 00:01:10.939
Island. "We’ll have a miserable morning’s work," Job told

11
00:01:10.940 --> 00:01:17.011
me. "We have to tow the ship with ropes for three miles to the haven behind Skeleton

12
00:01:17.012 --> 00:01:19.316
Island." "I’ll help," I

13
00:01:19.317 --> 00:01:25.199
volunteered. Job commanded my boat. Instead of keeping the crew calm, he grumbled the

14
00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:31.915
loudest. "Well, this misery won’t last forever," he said, then

15
00:01:31.916 --> 00:01:35.118
spat. I thought his behavior was a very bad

16
00:01:35.119 --> 00:01:41.463
sign. Up until now, the men had worked willingly, but once we sighted the island, they lost all

17
00:01:41.464 --> 00:01:47.371
discipline. While we towed the ship, Long John gave directions to the steersman, because he knew the area

18
00:01:47.372 --> 00:01:51.368
well. "Move quickly through here with the tide," he

19
00:01:51.369 --> 00:01:57.361
said. "The sea bottom is deep enough here to let a ship pass through

20
00:01:57.362 --> 00:02:01.207
easily." We dropped anchor at the spot where someone had drawn an anchor on the

21
00:02:01.208 --> 00:02:07.999
chart, about a third of a mile from each shore—the main island on one side and Skeleton Island on the

22
00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:14.032
other. The stagnant smell of sodden leaves and rotting tree trunks hung over

23
00:02:14.033 --> 00:02:23.432
everything. "I don’t know about treasure," the doctor said to the squire and the captain as he sniffed the air, "but I’ll bet there’s fever

24
00:02:23.433 --> 00:02:27.812
here." "Coil the ropes," the captain ordered as the men scrambled back on

25
00:02:27.813 --> 00:02:32.274
deck. The men gave him hostile looks and angrily

26
00:02:32.275 --> 00:02:38.835
obeyed. "Resentment is spreading to the honest men too," the doctor observed. "The entire crew is

27
00:02:38.836 --> 00:02:44.414
surly." "Mutiny hangs over us like a thundercloud," the squire whispered

28
00:02:44.415 --> 00:02:50.179
back. Long John, aware of the discontent, forced a smile onto his face and kept it

29
00:02:50.180 --> 00:02:55.686
there. He went about the ship setting a good example for the men, encouraging them to behave like real

30
00:02:55.687 --> 00:03:01.623
sailors. When the captain gave an order, Long John quickly got up with the cheeriest "Aye, aye,

31
00:03:01.624 --> 00:03:09.557
sir!" Later, when there was nothing else to do, he sang one song after another, as if to conceal the discontent of the men.

32
00:03:09.558 --> 00:03:14.407
"Silver’s nervousness is the worst omen of the day," the doctor said.

33
00:03:14.408 --> 00:03:21.739
We met in the cabin together with Trelawney’s men—Redruth, Hunter, and Joyce—who now knew about the pirate’s plans.

34
00:03:21.740 --> 00:03:26.554
Smollett told us, "If I give the men another order, the whole ship will revolt.

35
00:03:26.555 --> 00:03:30.286
We’ve only one man to rely on now."

36
00:03:30.287 --> 00:03:32.622
"Who?" the squire asked.

37
00:03:32.623 --> 00:03:39.592
"Silver," the captain said. "He’s as anxious as we are to keep this situation from exploding, because the time isn’t right.

38
00:03:39.593 --> 00:03:44.781
Let’s allow the men an afternoon ashore, and if they all go, we’ll control the ship.

39
00:03:44.782 --> 00:03:54.929
If none go, we’ll hold the cabin. If some go, you mark my words, sir, Silver will bring them aboard again as mild as lambs."

40
00:03:54.930 --> 00:04:00.905
Captain Smollett gave all the loyal men pistols, and then went on deck to address the crew.

41
00:04:00.906 --> 00:04:04.999
"My lads," he said, "it’s hot, and we’re all tired.

42
00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:12.435
Some time ashore will hurt nobody, and since the boats are still in the water, take them and go ashore for the afternoon.

43
00:04:12.436 --> 00:04:17.836
I’ll fire a gun half an hour before sundown as a signal to return."

44
00:04:17.837 --> 00:04:22.319
"Hooray!" they cheered, their bad mood vanishing.

45
00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:28.826
"The silly fellows must think they’ll trip over treasure as soon as they land," the squire muttered.

46
00:04:28.827 --> 00:04:35.160
Then Smollett quickly went below, leaving Long John to arrange who would go ashore.

47
00:04:35.161 --> 00:04:41.167
If the captain had remained on deck, he could no longer have pretended not to understand the situation,

48
00:04:41.168 --> 00:04:45.089
because it was plain that Long John was their real captain.

49
00:04:45.090 --> 00:04:52.065
At last, Long John decided six fellows would stay on board; he and the rest would leave the ship.

50
00:04:52.066 --> 00:04:57.204
Right then, a crazy notion, perhaps the one that saved us, came into my head.

51
00:04:57.205 --> 00:05:03.319
If six pirates were left, I knew our party could not take over the ship.

52
00:05:03.320 --> 00:05:10.322
But since there were only six, the cabin party had no need of my assistance, so I decided to go ashore.

53
00:05:10.323 --> 00:05:16.589
Quickly, I slipped over the side, curled up under the canvas on the nearest boat, then shoved off.

54
00:05:16.590 --> 00:05:24.065
No one took notice of me except Alan Jenkins, who said, "Is that you, Jim? Keep your head down."

55
00:05:24.066 --> 00:05:29.556
Long John looked sharply over from the other boat. "Is Jim going ashore too?"

56
00:05:29.557 --> 00:05:33.476
From that moment, I began to regret what I had done.

57
00:05:33.477 --> 00:05:41.792
The shore boats raced for the beach, but the boat I was in, having started first and being lighter and better manned, shot far ahead.

58
00:05:41.793 --> 00:05:50.091
Once the boat neared the trees along the shore, I caught an overhanging branch, swung myself out, and plunged into the nearest thicket.

59
00:05:50.092 --> 00:05:53.809
Long John and the rest were still a hundred yards behind.

60
00:05:53.810 --> 00:05:57.689
"Jim, Jim!" I heard him shouting.

61
00:05:57.690 --> 00:06:07.169
But I paid no heed. Jumping and ducking, I followed my nose and ran until I could run no longer.

